“Appropriate frame is arguably the least-specified part of the model as it deals with choosing the right level in the decision maker’s system of values. Value systems are known to be hierarchical with potentially infinite number of meta-levels. As Hartman convincingly demonstrates in [5], values are best understood as formal relations (correspondences) between outcome properties and decision maker’s intentions.”
“Di culty in inferring such intentions is the most likely reason why the academic literature on framing avoids a prescriptive stance on values, and instead assumes that values are already explicated, or can be conversation-elicited otherwise [6].”
“an expert is employed to identify (at least) a few most obvious variables of chance that can potentially be related to an outcome of interest. Next, follow-up questions are designed about uncertainties that can also be relevant to this initial set and/or to the values of interest. In this fashion, the list of the state variables is gradually expanded while the scope of the problem grows wider. Such workflow usually poses little initial di culty in the business environment, where the variables related to sales and profits are usually trivial to identify.”
“working towards a comprehensive list of uncertainties and observations in this workflow often appears much harder because (as we mentioned before), it forces the expert to hallucinate the creative decision alternatives, the requisite uncertainties, and future value components at the same time.”
“it is often di cult to think straight through the many uncertainties that occur infrequently but may have far-reaching consequences – like natural disasters, epidemics, and landslide shifts in political administrations and regulatory environments”
