“Psychologists have been studying decisionmaking for more than 30 years. During this time, two research strategies have predominated. The earliest of these, which may be called the prescriptive strategy, involves translating a formal, typically normative model of behavior drawn from statistics or economics into an experimental task. Naive subjects are then asked to perform the task, and their behavior is compared with the optimal behavior prescribed by the model. Most often, subjects fare poorly, not only in terms of the quantitative fit between their behavior and optimal behavior, but also in qualitative terms, evidenced by persistent a perverse violation of fundamental and seemingly self-evident axioms of rational behavior. In recent years, the prescriptive strategy has fallen on hard times, and it is generally agreed [21], [91] that normative models provide a poor basis for positive or descriptive psychological theory.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 764)
“Current thinking favors instead a descriptive strategy, dubbed the "information processing" or "cognitive" approach. Research in the descriptive mode focuses on the processes through which decisions are actually generated.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 764)
“ypically, the research outcome is an explanation in psychological terms of why human behavior deviates from the optimal. Most often these explanations center on the shortcuts or heuristics that people use to help them get along in a world whose complexity far exceeds their limited information processing capabilities [41]. Although the prescriptive and the descriptive strategies are generally taken to be contrasting, they share the assumption that normative models tell us both what subjects ought to be doing and what they would be doing were it not for unavoidable cognitive limitations. Thus, both strategies focus, for the most part, on people's inadequacies relative to a normative standard. The result is a curiously off-center psychology in which people are seen not as figure, but as ground; not as what they are, but as what they are not.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 764)
“Although the measure of scientific achievement is ordinarily found in the increasing rigor and formalism of the language in which theory is expressed, it seems that in the case of psychological decision theory, movement is needed in the opposite direction. Due largely to our deep involvement with normative models, decision researchers face a peculiar and most unfortunate situation in which the formal structures of our theories are advanced mathematically well beyond the point justified by what we have to convey descriptively about the substance of human decisionmaking activity.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 764)
“Clear effort is needed towards describing the decisionmaker and the decisionmaking environment in theoretical terms that do not prejudge either the goals that the decisionmaker is pursuing or the efficiency of the pursuit.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 764)
“here are several critical areas in which research should proceed. One of these is to relate decisionmaking to the basic psychological constructs that form the foundation for modern information processing analysis and cognitive psychology in studies that involve processes such as attention, perception, mental representation, and inference. Although the term information processing model has been appearing in decision making articles regularly since Slovic and Lichtenstein's 1968 seminal paper, the term has not, in general, referred to the kind of procedural analysis that characterizes most research in human information processing. As a consequence, critical constructs in decision models are often used without either theoretical or operational meaning. For example, the concept of information "weight" is ubiquitous throughout decision research. Presumably it reflects, either as cause or effect, the attention that is paid to information. Yet, it does not appear possible to find studies which explicitly try using attentional measures to help hammer out the relationship between attention and weight. This is a critical need at this time.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 764)
“A second area in need of additional research relates to the construction of psychological decision models that incorporate humanly relevant aspects of experience. For example, in the area of risky decisionmaking, the theoretical terms that most psychologists use tend to be devoid of psychological meaning. Thus, the term risk aversion generally refers only to the shape of the utility function that can describe a person's preferences among gambles. Nowhere is this term related to other theoretically relevant terms (e.g., "risk" as it is defined in portfolio theory [55], nor is the term linked to relevant emotional labels such as fear, hope, safety, danger, or thrill, although some economists [1], [26] [74] appear willing to consider such factors.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 764)
“Finally, computational studies are needed of the decision rules that are believed to be commonly used by naive human beings. Through these, it might be possible to discover, by computer simulation or mathematical analysis, how these rules operate in various environments in terms of their efficiency, their robustness, their weaknesses, and their strengths. In some sense, of course, this will require that we attempt to characterize the environment in which decisionmaking takes place. But to do so is no more than to recognize that psychological research cannot be performed in a vacuum, physical or theoretical.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 764)
“Values are indispensible and unavoidable elements in decisionmaking and evaluation processes. Values enter decisions in various forms: in the creation of alternatives, as inclinations or preferences for alternatives, as trade-offs among the implications of the alternatives, or as value commitments of the decisionmaker. Most decisionmakers take their values into account implicitly and informally. In many instances, however, it is important and useful to consider values explicitly and formally. For example, when the decision is complex and mistakes can be costly, when trade-offs are crucial and painful, when new alternatives can be created, or when the decision needs to be accounted for, then explicit and formal processes for structuring and analyzing values are often preferable to implicit and informal uses.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 765)
“the prior step necessary in value analysis; namely, identifying and structuring of the dimensions of value that are important to a problem, an individual, or an organization.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 765)
“Value analysis uses a variety of procedures and theories from business, engineering, and the social sciences. The values of an individual or group pertaining to possible decisions are expressed in responses to questions such as "What are your main objectives and concerns relating to the decision problem? What attributes or measures would be appropriate for differentiating between options? Why is a specific alternative either good or bad, desirable, or undesirable?" Using the responses to these questions, the analyst identifies and structures a hierarchy of values, referred to as a value tree, with general values and concerns at the top and specific criteria at the bottom” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 765)
“The top-down approach is based on an explication of the evaluators' most general values bearing on decisions of concern. The analyst asks the evaluators for their overall values first. Next, evaluators are asked to explain the meanings of their initial value categories and their interrelationships. The discussion may indicate, for instance, that environmental damage is a major objective for evaluating energy policies. The analyst pursues this by asking what is meant by environmental damage, why is it important, how it is affected by energy options, and so on. The result of such an interview is a list of concerns articulated with logical principles but not in a structured manner. In the interview, it is not necessary to worry about consistency or double counting; it is more important to stretch the imagination to promite a wide spectrum of values. Part of the explication process is an exercise in semantics, inducing a significant clarification of meaning and understanding.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 765)
“his initial list is then reduced by eliminating criteria that are not value relevant and expanded by including criteria for any " holes" that can be identified. Next, groups of related criteria are aggregated into a higher-level value category. This clustering process is repeated for higher level values until a small number of general value areas is identified that covers the range of the original criteria list. Having arrived at a preliminary value tree structure—either with the top-down or the bottom-up method—the analyst can appraise the tree structure by checking whether 1) criteria explain the meaning of a general value category or relate in some other way (e.g., functional, means-end); 2) any criterion is included in more than one higher-level category; 3) the list of criteria is exhausti” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 765)
“Having arrived at a preliminary value tree structure—either with the top-down or the bottom-up method—the analyst can appraise the tree structure by checking whether 1) criteria explain the meaning of a general value category or relate in some other way (e.g., functional, means-end); 2) any criterion is included in more than one higher-level category; 3) the list of criteria is exhaustive; and 4) any criterion is redundant.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 765)
“Identification of objectives, structuring value trees, construction of scales to measure objectives, and assessing utility and value functions offer mechanisms to focus on several value-related issues in decisions. Since values are the reason for any interest in a decision, value analysis is useful to guide all aspects of analysis, such as creating alternatives and evaluating options, bargaining and negotiation to help reach a consensus, deciding what information to collect, and communicating important aspects of a decision problem.” (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 765)
