	url = {https://www.jstor.org/stable/3665006},
	doi = {10.2307/3665006},
	abstract = {Conventional capital budgeting techniques lead to a rejection of investments with a negative present value (leaving out portfolio effects). This paper argues that in a situation where there is uncertainty and the possibility of replicating the investment, one should not always reject an investment with a negative present value without further analysis. There may be sufficient reason to try the investment if there is some probability that the investment will turn out to have a positive present value. It is necessary to do the computations to determine if the "undesirable" investment should be undertaken in order to obtain information that might lead to undertaking the multiple investments.},
