	author = {Pakath, R},
	year = {1988},
	annote = {“A central paradigm for decision support, fundamental to disciplines such as operations research and management science (OR/MS), is to construct and solve mathematical models of real-world decision problems. Mathematical models can be broadly categorized as deterministic models and stochastic (or probabilistic) models operating in either a static or a dynamic environment” (Pakath, 1988, p. 21)

“Deterministic models of a decision problem assume that the parameters of the real problem (such as cost data, capacity and demand parameters, and other such inputs to a model of the real problem) are known with certainty. Conversely, stochastic models assume that some or perhaps all of the parameter values are uncertain. The operating environment is regarded as being static if it is unchanging, or remains stationary, for extended periods of time or at least during the period of the analysis (i.e., the time required for model building, model solution, decision making and decision implementation). Therefore, in a static environment the true parameter values of a decision problem are also believed to be stationary during the period under consideration. In a dynamic environment, on the other hand, some or all of the parameter values could perhaps be changing with the passage of time.” (Pakath, 1988, p. 21)

“ecision makers are often faced with making decisions based on phenomena that have uncertainty associated with them. There are several models in the operations research literature that address the issue of uncertainty. The general approach suggested is to study the uncertain phenomena to see if they exhibit some degree of regularity that can be described by probability models. Again in the context of inventory control, we have the stochastic single-period inventory models. Such models assume that product demand during the period is a random variable having a known probability distribution.” (Pakath, 1988, p. 22)

