@phdthesis{tolley_information_1989,
	title = {Information used in determining mode of communication for preschool hearing handicapped students - {ProQuest}},
	url = {https://www.proquest.com/docview/303699550?pq-origsite=gscholar&fromopenview=true&sourcetype=Dissertations%20&%20Theses},
	abstract = {Information used in determining mode of communication for preschool hearing handicapped students
Tolley, Nancy Purdue.   University of Cincinnati ProQuest Dissertations Publishing,  1989. 9003228.

Five groups of public school professionals (school psychologists, educational audiologists, speech/language pathologists, special education teachers, special education administrators) were asked to recommend a mode of communication (oral/aural or Total Communication) for two hypothetical deaf preschool children. Information from existing decision-making guidelines [Deafness Management Quotient DMQ (Downs, 1974), revised Deafness Management Quotient-DMQ-R (Luterman \& Chasin, 1981) and Spoken Language Predictor Index-SLP (Geers \& Moog, 1987)] were incorporated into the case studies. Fifty-five professionals participated in the study (n = 10 for all groups except the teachers - 10 preschool teachers, 5 parent-infant specialists). The Rimoldi technique (Rimoldi, 1955) was used to analyze patterns of information selected by participants and variations in the selection patterns of specialist groups. Participants' actual data selection and recommendations also were compared with existing guidelines (DMQ, DMQ-R, SLP). Results indicated information from aided audiograms, language samples and the family commitment to a mode of communication were chosen most frequently. Chronological age, pure tone and aided audiogram results, age of onset, and age of first hearing aid use were the highest ranking items of information selected overall. The variations in the selection patterns among specialist groups reflected their areas of expertise; however, specialists also chose items R eproduced with perm ission of the copyright ow ner. Further reproduction prohibited without perm ission

supporting a multifactored and multidisciplinary approach to the basis of their decision making. Comparisons between the actual data selection patterns and recommendations for the two cases and those predicted by the DMQy DMQ-R, and SLP indicated poor correspondence between the participants' behaviors and those predicted by the formulae. These results raise questions about the utility of the existing formulae in providing practical guidelines for decision making. In conclusion, professionals chose information which supported a developmental, time-referenced approach to decision making. The child's use of language and best levels of hearing were stressed, as were family variables. Participants ranked descriptive data over summary data, supporting the importance of non-test based assessment and observation. Further research into making this type of decision, and exploration of the impact of personal and professional bias on decision making are needed.},
