 More precisely, the modification of the usual axioms is not necessary, since the emphasis on inequalities, essential though it is, can be regarded as belonging to the rules of application of the abstract or mathematical theory instead of to the abstract theory itself [3], [4]. In this way the whole subject is kept very simple and easy to understand, in its essence, without much mathematical background, although complications always arise in applications. Measure theory is not essential.” (Good, 1962, p. image 3)“Distinction between "Decision Theory" and the Theory of Rationality My interpretation of the theory of rationality is somewhat clarified by saying in what way it differs from "decision theory" as that expression is normally used. It is usually regarded as part of the definition of "decision theory" that all the possible decisions should be listed in advance of the observations. This device is not at all an essential part of the theory of rationality. Sometimes it pays to list the possible decisions in advance of the observations and sometimes it does not pay. It is something that has to be judged on each occasion.” (Good, 1962, p. image 3)“Tukey [28] argued that, in addition to a decision theory, one needs a conclusion theory. But a theory of rationality is supposed to encompass them both: it is just as much a theory of conclusions as a theory of decisions and of rational actions.” (Good, 1962, p. image 4)“a decision is a mental event that causes an act; more precisely a decision is a conscious mental event that has a tendency to cause either an act or to cause another mental event to belong to some class rather than to another clas” (Good, 1962, p. image 4)“A conclussion is a decision to accept a hypothesis at least provisionally, where the word "hypothesis" is to be understood in a very general sense. In the particular case when the hypothesis is that one course of action is better than another one, then the conclusion is liable to lead at once to a decision that is not a conclusion, namely the decision to perform the preferab” (Good, 1962, p. image 4)“A conclussion is a decision to accept a hypothesis at least provisionally, where the word "hypothesis" is to be understood in a very general sense. In the particular case when the hypothesis is that one course of action is better than another one, then the conclusion is liable to lead at once to a decision that is not a conclusion, namely the decision to perform the preferable course of action. But sometimes we do not have the courage of our convictions.” (Good, 1962, p. image 4)“Not all mental events are caused immediately and primarily by a conscious mental event; for if they were we could prove by backward induction that we must be conscious all the time, including before we were conceived. Our decisions are necessarily determined in part by unconscious mental events, by physiological events and by external forces; and it is impossible to make conscious use of the theory of rationality for all our decisions, before the decisions are made.” (Good, 1962, p. image 4)“e made. Whether or not our decisions have been thought out in terms of the theory of rationality, we can often learn something by analyzing our past decisions to see how consistent they were. The analysis is known as a "post-mortem."” (Good, 1962, p. image 4)“3.5 Degrees of Rationality An unconscious man can be completely consistent, but his set of judgments is of zero utility. A conscious man can be only more or less consistent, in other words there are degrees of consistency or of rationality. This is an idea that can be given a highly tentative definition in terms of the utility of a set or body of judgments, 3. If we assume that every one of our acts has an expected utility, then, in particular, the act of expressing a body, 3, of judgments, has an expected utility, U. It can easily happen that the expected utility would have been larger if some of the judgments had been omitted, for example, if the o