@phdthesis{sherman_market_1989,
	address = {Hawaii},
	title = {Market failure and the underprovision of parks and protected areas},
	url = {https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/2abbd515-6c88-4348-b507-8e10ca38fe0d/content},
	abstract = {The indiscriminate ccnver-saon and development of the world's remaining natural areas (i.e., areas that remain relatively undisturbed by man and close to their natural state) are considered to be crucial problems facing society. Local impacts include effects on soil, water and local climate. More widespread effe{\textasciitilde}ts include implications for global climate and increases in the loss of biodiversity (short for biological diversity which includes all species, genetic variation within species and all varieties of habitats and ecosystems). Failure to protect enough area may result in large losses in social welfare to both current and future generations. One way of preventing these potential losses is by giving valuable natural areas protected status, i.e., establishing them as parks, nature reserves, wildlife sanctuaries or some other form of protected area. This dissertation examines the hypothesis that, as a result of market failures, there is a bias toward conversion and development of natural areas. The effect of this bias is that a smaller amount of area is protected or left in its natural state than would be the case if there were a full accounting of all the societal benefits and costs associated with each alternative land use. Three types of market failure are considered: market failures leading to an underestimation of the benefits of protection; market failures which make it difficult for governments to capture the i

benefits of protection; and the related problems of uncertainty, irreversibility and intergenerational equity. Each of these manifestations of market failure is discussed in detail, and means of overcoming them are presented. Estimation of benefits and the proportion of benefits captured can both be improved by applying economic theory and methods; dealing with uncertainty and irreversibility is more difficult. An example of how to value some of the benefits and costs of protected areas is presented using Khao Yai National Park in Thailand as a case study.},
	language = {en},
	urldate = {2024-05-03},
	school = {UNIVERSITY OF HAWAII},
	author = {Sherman, Paul Barry},
	year = {1989},
	keywords = {read CW},
	annote = {Annotations(5/20/2024, 9:48:41 PM)
“as a result of market failures, there is a bias toward conversion and development of natural areas. The effect of this bias is that a smaller amount of area is left in its natural state than would be the case if there were a full accounting of all the societal benefits and costs associated with each alternative land use.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 15)“the benefits provided by protecting natural areas extend far beyond their boundaries, i.e., there are externalities involved. The social benefits accrue to a wide number of people, though the benefits to each person are often relatively small. Thus, the benefits to the private entity from developing the area often far outweigh the costs he must bear, although the total social cost including off-site benefits may be higher than the total private benefit.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 15)“In the absence of incentives to do so, the private entity has no reason to incorporate the external costs of his action on others” (Sherman, 1989, p. 15)“Like other types of externalities, government intervention may be needed to overcome the market failure and maintain efficiency conditions. Tresch (1981) states that government intervention is required if: 1) an activity by one entity affects the utility of others not directly involved with the activity, and 2) the resulting gains and losses are not accurately reflected in the market.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 16)“Decisions to exploit natural areas, on the surface at least, often appear to be in the national interest for many developing economies.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 17)“Deciding how much land to protect, which lands to protect, and which uses to allow poses difficult questions. In many developing countries, the demand for agricultural land and/or the value of the resources contained in natural areas provide strong\_.!!!O.tivations for governments to allow or even actively encourage the conversion and development of such areas.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 20)“the benefits from protecting natural areas are more diffuse and more likely to be spread out over long periods of time.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 20)“the benefits from protecting natural areas are more diffuse and more likely to be spread out over long periods of time. When given the choice between large immediate benefits from development and long-term, less certain and less quantifiable benefits from conservation, many governments are choosing the former.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 20)“the decision making view taken will be that of a social planner who attempts to maximize social welfare.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 21)“If information might be gained by delaying an irreversible decision, the expected value of that information has been termed quasi-option value (Conrad 1980).” (Sherman, 1989, p. 42)“The actual weighing of benefits and costs when evaluating alternatives can take several forms. If estimates of both benefits and the associated costs are known, some form of a benefit-cost analysis (BCA) can be carried out. The techniques for this are well known and involve the evaluation of a stream of benefits and costs over some chosen period of time.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 44)“Another approach to these decisions is known as the safe minimum standard (SMS) approach originally developed by Ciriacy-Wantrup (1952) and also advocated by Bishop (1978). In essence, the SMS approach uses a modified version of the "minimax" criterion; this involves choosing the alternative which minimizes the maximum possible loss that could result from making the wrong decision. In the modified approach, this alternative is chosen unless the costs of doing so are "unacceptably large" (Bishop 1978).” (Sherman, 1989, p. 44)“Another possible approach to these types of decisions is to use cost-effectiveness analysis (CEA). This approach does not attempt to value benefits; it focuses on identifying the least-cost method 31 ------------ ---------- --” (Sherman, 1989, p. 44)“of reaching a desired goal (e.g. protection of a certain number of hectares of a specific habitat).” (Sherman, 1989, p. 45)“Originally developed in the United States to evaluate water resource projects, BCA is now widely used in project formulation and evaluation. Its application to the issue of protecting natural areas was first discussed by John Krutilla in his seminal article, "Conservation Reconsidered" (Krutilla 1967). Prior to this, various economists had addressed individual issues that enter into the protection/development decision, but none had directly pursued this topic. Perhaps the only other economist who considered many of the relevant issues before this was Ciriacy-Wantrup (see Ciriacy-Wantrup 1952).” (Sherman, 1989, p. 45)“though the opportunity costs of each individual decision may not be large, the cumulative costs of such a policy may be substantial.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 54)“the actual benefits of protection are greater than the measurable benefits, and that the decisionmaker must weigh the unknown, unquantifiable benefits against the opportunity costs of not developing the area. In the SMS framework, the decisionmaker must weigh near-term costs against uncertain future costs.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 55)“in some situations, decisions must be made with a "jUdgment call." The fundamental reason for this lack of a clearcut decision criterion is the presence of market failures which results in an inability to accurately measure current and future benefits of protection” (Sherman, 1989, p. 55)“In the absence of government intervention, many important watershed protection areas would be lost. Another example might be East African game parks--government support may be profitable in terms of attracting tourists and the wide range of associated tourist expenditures which then occur both within and outside protected areas. In most cases, however, these benefits would not be included by a private decision maker since he does not directly benefit from them.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 57)“In its simplest sense, an irreversible change is one which is permanent and cannot be altered. Economists, however, tend to view irreversibility in a slightly different fashion. Some consider a decision to be irreversible if it significantly reduces future options for a long period of time (Henry 1974). Miller and Lad (1984) speak of irreversibility as a constraint in that it limits the range of actions which can be taken subsequently. In this manner, an irreversible decision can be viewed as a potential decrease in welfare--a constrained optimal decision cannot be superior to an unconstrained one.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 70)“In a sense, any decision, once implemented, is irreversible. The key factors in the context of protected areas, however, are that the effects are often permanent and potentially substantial. While less important decisions may also be irreversible, when found to be incorrect ex post, such decisions can often be adjusted with only short term and insignificant effects. In the protected area context, however, it may not be possible to make adjustments to incorrect decisions, thereby leading to losses that continue in perpetuity.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 71)“since future generations have no political representation, potentially irreversible damages done to fUture generations would not be included in decision making. The cost of these irreversibilities are "shifted" to fUture generations without consideration by current decision makers.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 72)“When an irreversible development is undertaken, future alternatives become limited or are foreclosed. As a result, there is a value in delaying a decision which involves irreversible effects whose values are not known. This value, which arises from delaying such an irreversible decision, has been termed "quasi-option value" (Arrow and Fisher 1974) and can be characterized as the expected value of information that might be gained by delaying an irreversible decision (Conrad 1980).” (Sherman, 1989, p. 73)“For example, if there is uncertainty about the future value of benefits from protecting an area and more information can be gained by delaying development, there is a value in delaying the decision while more information is gathered (or generated by research). The magnitude of the quasi-option value will be larger the larger the potential loss, the more likely this loss will occur, the smaller the opportunity costs of preserving future options and the lower the discount rate (Henry 1974; Freeman 1984). (It should be noted, however, that development may also generate quasi-option value if it leads to better information for future decisions [Freeman 1984; Miller and Lad 1984]).” (Sherman, 1989, p. 73)“Both uncertainty and irreversibility are entwined with the issue of intergenerational eqUity. While irreversibility can cause adverse consequences both to the current generation or to some future generation, the permanent nature of incorrect decisions which give rise to irreversible effects will always be felt by future generations.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 74)“While uncertainty can sometimes be handled by using expected values, it has been shown that, in decisions involving irreversible consequences, use of expected values will not result in optimal decisions (Arrow and Fisher 1974)” (Sherman, 1989, p. 75)“This simplistic depiction of land allocation ignores the market failures which pervade the protected area decision process. It shows what would occur if decisions were made by a social planner under conditions of certainty, perfect information and reversibility.” (Sherman, 1989, p. 84)
},
	file = {content.pdf:/Users/corywhitney/Zotero/storage/V9TH479J/content.pdf:application/pdf},
}

